## Chapter 3: The Dome Structure

The dome is not the first structure of Dome‑World.

The landscape came first.  
The hydrology came second.  
The dome comes third.

The architecture does not conquer the land; it settles into it. The earth provides thermal and structural stability. The dome provides atmospheric enclosure. Together, they create a boundary that is both heavily sheltered and radically open to the surrounding world.

### The Compression Ring

Every dome begins with a circle. The circular geometry distributes lateral forces evenly; no single wall carries more structural burden than another. Wind slips around the curvature rather than stalling against a flat surface. Snow settles in predictable loads. The geometry performs mechanical work before materials are ever added.

The lowest structural element is a continuous compression ring anchored to the prepared earthen terrace. This ring transfers the dome’s entire weight and outward thrust smoothly into the earthworks below. It acts as the universal anchor for the frame, the exterior membrane, the interior suspension, and the drainage catchment. The ring remains visible; the foundation of the house must always be legible to its inhabitants.

### The Modular Frame

The dome lattice is constructed from repeating, interconnected members—bamboo, laminated timber, or lightweight steel, depending on local ecology. The engineering principle is reciprocal load distribution: no member is structural destiny. Every component is designed to be unbolted and replaced without dismantling the whole. A building that cannot be dismantled piece‑by‑piece cannot survive generations.

### The Permeable Skin

The outer membrane protects the frame from precipitation while breathing to prevent interior condensation. Treated canvas, natural vulcanized fabrics, or repairable synthetics stretch over the lattice. The membrane is expected to age. It is designed to be patched, stitched, and recoated rather than discarded. Visible repair is not damage; it is the physical history of care.

### Rain as Telemetry

Precipitation is welcomed. The dome’s curvature directs rainfall precisely into gutters that feed the settlement’s broader hydraulic network. Every drop follows a visible, engineered path. Rain is never hidden blindly behind drywall; inhabitants can always trace where water lands, travels, and exits. The hydrology of the house acts as real‑time environmental telemetry.

### Building as Teacher

The dome teaches through its form. A child sees rain collected rather than wasted. A visitor understands immediately that structural loads travel downward into the earth. The house explains its own physics simply by standing. That is good engineering.

